1
00:00:00,255 --> 00:00:02,898
Here’s a small bug that makes a useful
full walkthrough.

2
00:00:03,163 --> 00:00:07,394
The order validator accepts zero. The
issue says an order needs at least one

3
00:00:07,405 --> 00:00:07,695
item.

4
00:00:08,020 --> 00:00:11,019
We’ll trace the cause in Copilot Plan,
make the change in Agent,

5
00:00:11,130 --> 00:00:12,466
and show both test runs.

6
00:00:12,888 --> 00:00:15,025
Then we’ll hand the pull request to a
teammate,

7
00:00:15,176 --> 00:00:17,795
who reviews it with GitHub CLI.

8
00:00:18,983 --> 00:00:22,081
Open the training issue. We want one and
five to pass,

9
00:00:22,352 --> 00:00:24,388
and zero and negative one to fail.

10
00:00:24,859 --> 00:00:27,368
We also want to keep the existing
function signature.

11
00:00:27,695 --> 00:00:31,889
Those details make the work easy to
check. For your own training repository,

12
00:00:32,060 --> 00:00:34,455
use the actual issue number GitHub
assigned.

13
00:00:34,816 --> 00:00:36,797
Seventeen is the number in this example.

14
00:00:37,755 --> 00:00:41,442
In VS Code, open Copilot Chat and select
Plan when it’s available.

15
00:00:41,784 --> 00:00:44,733
Add the issue, the validator, and the
existing tests.

16
00:00:45,305 --> 00:00:48,260
Ask why zero gets through and which test
would expose it.

17
00:00:48,611 --> 00:00:52,411
The plan should point to the equality in
the return line and propose a new zero

18
00:00:52,411 --> 00:00:52,766
test.

19
00:00:53,187 --> 00:00:56,142
Read both files and agree on the scope
before changing them.

20
00:00:57,170 --> 00:01:01,440
Switch to Agent and ask for the test
first. Inspect the edit and confirm the

21
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:03,307
source comparison has stayed the same.

22
00:01:03,668 --> 00:01:06,835
Run the approved test command on a
checkout with that edit.

23
00:01:07,305 --> 00:01:11,485
Find the line for the zero test. We
expected this failure because the old

24
00:01:11,485 --> 00:01:13,683
implementation still returns true.

25
00:01:14,113 --> 00:01:17,476
If you see an import error instead, sort
out the test setup first.

26
00:01:18,463 --> 00:01:21,225
Now make the one-line fix. When we
remove equality,

27
00:01:21,356 --> 00:01:23,395
zero no longer satisfies the condition.

28
00:01:23,856 --> 00:01:27,059
One and five still do, and negative one
still fails it.

29
00:01:27,570 --> 00:01:31,662
Run the same suite in the approved
environment. The result should say four

30
00:01:31,662 --> 00:01:33,002
tests ran and passed.

31
00:01:33,473 --> 00:01:37,330
Keep the red and green results together
so your teammate can see what the new

32
00:01:37,330 --> 00:01:38,057
test proved.

33
00:01:39,045 --> 00:01:43,407
Open Source Control in VS Code. Check
the validator diff for the comparison

34
00:01:43,407 --> 00:01:47,027
change, then open the test diff and find
the zero assertion.

35
00:01:47,498 --> 00:01:49,456
Check that no unrelated file came along.

36
00:01:49,887 --> 00:01:53,823
Commit these two changes on the training
feature branch and publish the branch

37
00:01:53,823 --> 00:01:54,991
through Source Control.

38
00:01:55,272 --> 00:01:58,331
You now have a small patch, with each
change tied to the issue.

39
00:01:59,213 --> 00:02:03,580
In the approved remote terminal, use an
authorized author account and a checkout

40
00:02:03,580 --> 00:02:04,618
of the published branch.

41
00:02:04,978 --> 00:02:08,154
Prepare a short pull request body.
Include the change,

42
00:02:08,304 --> 00:02:11,019
the red and green test evidence, and the
issue link.

43
00:02:11,590 --> 00:02:17,130
Open the pull request with GitHub CLI.
Our example receives number forty-two.

44
00:02:17,506 --> 00:02:20,416
Give that number to a separate
authorized teammate.

45
00:02:21,318 --> 00:02:24,385
The reviewer uses their own account in
the approved terminal.

46
00:02:24,666 --> 00:02:28,824
Read the pull request, inspect the diff,
and check the required results for that

47
00:02:28,824 --> 00:02:29,350
revision.

48
00:02:29,761 --> 00:02:33,380
If the evidence supports approval, the
human reviewer records it.

49
00:02:33,831 --> 00:02:36,433
If something is missing, they request a
specific change.

50
00:02:36,819 --> 00:02:40,818
This is the teammate’s decision. We’ll
practice both outcomes in the dedicated

51
00:02:40,818 --> 00:02:41,554
review lesson.

52
00:02:42,547 --> 00:02:46,345
Look back at what you can hand over.
There’s an issue with clear inputs,

53
00:02:46,675 --> 00:02:50,673
a small diff, a test that failed then
passed, and a peer decision.

54
00:02:51,164 --> 00:02:53,683
Try the same workflow on another
synthetic issue.

55
00:02:54,113 --> 00:02:57,553
Replace the example identifiers with
your own training issue and pull

56
00:02:57,553 --> 00:02:58,020
request.

57
00:02:58,482 --> 00:03:01,068
Ask your teammate to tell you which
evidence they used.
